WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.894
Phileas Fogg and his eighty-day adventure were soon overshadowed by other events.

2
00:00:05.894 --> 00:00:11.744
A report appeared describing the "gentleman" robber who had stolen the fifty-five-thousand pounds,

3
00:00:11.744 --> 00:00:16.547
and the newspapers were caught up once again with the details of the robbery.

4
00:00:16.547 --> 00:00:20.532
The police were looking for suspects all over Europe.

5
00:00:20.532 --> 00:00:28.254
One agent in the vast manhunt was a short, slightly built man with a nervous, intelligent face and bright eyes.

6
00:00:28.254 --> 00:00:34.176
Detective Fix had been sent to Suez to watch every person who arrived there.

7
00:00:34.176 --> 00:00:41.973
His instructions were to investigate any suspicious characters and certainly anyone who resembled the thief.

8
00:00:41.973 --> 00:00:45.444
Fix stood on the dock, looking out at the water.

9
00:00:45.444 --> 00:00:50.583
His eyebrows twitched nonstop, which was always a sign of his impatience.

10
00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:54.544
"Excuse me," Fix called to a passing sailor.

11
00:00:54.544 --> 00:00:59.009
"Can you tell me if the Mongolia is expected on time?"

12
00:00:59.009 --> 00:01:03.459
"It's a very punctual ship, it is," replied the sailor.

13
00:01:03.459 --> 00:01:06.633
"It has won awards for its speed."

14
00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:11.008
Fix felt that he had a good sense for spotting criminals.

15
00:01:11.008 --> 00:01:14.058
He had arrested plenty in his time.

16
00:01:14.058 --> 00:01:20.151
If the thief were on board the Mongolia, he wouldn't sneak past Fix.

17
00:01:20.151 --> 00:01:24.039
"What a magnificent robbery it was," Fix thought.

18
00:01:24.039 --> 00:01:30.536
"Fifty-five-thousand pounds! And by a man who resembles an honest gentleman.

19
00:01:30.536 --> 00:01:36.419
It's extraordinary! Thieves are getting to be bold nowadays.

20
00:01:36.419 --> 00:01:42.843
But then those fellows never have rascally faces, or they would be arrested immediately.

21
00:01:42.843 --> 00:01:48.941
No, I'm sure this thief looks honest, and I'm going to find him." The

22
00:01:48.941 --> 00:01:57.370
Mongolia was stopping in Suez for only a few hours to refuel, so Fix did not have a lot of time to spot his man. Fix

23
00:01:57.370 --> 00:02:05.608
took his position, and when the ship arrived and the passengers disembarked, he carefully examined every face that passed. One

24
00:02:05.608 --> 00:02:11.661
of the passengers vigorously pushed his way through the crowd and came right up to Fix. The

25
00:02:11.661 --> 00:02:19.077
stranger held out a passport, explaining that he wished to have it stamped, and asked directions to the British consulate. Fix

26
00:02:19.077 --> 00:02:23.773
instinctively took the passport and read the description of its owner. His

27
00:02:23.773 --> 00:02:29.605
eyebrows lifted in surprise. The description perfectly matched the bank thief. "Is

28
00:02:29.605 --> 00:02:33.320
this your passport?" asked Fix. The

29
00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:39.412
stranger was Passepartout, and the passport in his hand belonged to Phileas Fogg. "No,

30
00:02:39.412 --> 00:02:42.921
it's my master's," replied Passepartout. "And

31
00:02:42.921 --> 00:02:46.087
where is your master now?" asked Fix. "He

32
00:02:46.087 --> 00:02:49.001
stayed onboard," said Passepartout. "He

33
00:02:49.001 --> 00:02:53.394
must go to the consul's office in person," said Fix. "And

34
00:02:53.394 --> 00:02:59.622
where is the consulate? My master won't be pleased to be disturbed," replied Passepartout, frowning. "The

35
00:02:59.622 --> 00:03:10.274
consulate is not far. Have you or your master been to Suez before?" asked Fix, hoping for more information about the owner of the passport. "I

36
00:03:10.274 --> 00:03:15.626
have not," said Passepartout. "And I do not expect to see much of it. In

37
00:03:15.626 --> 00:03:19.620
fact, I expected my master to stop in Paris to see the sights. But

38
00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:24.577
all I saw of that city was seen as we rushed from one train station to another." "You

39
00:03:24.577 --> 00:03:29.920
are in a great hurry then?" asked Fix, growing more curious. "I

40
00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.730
am not, but my master is," said Passepartout. "By

41
00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:36.721
the way, I must buy some shirts and shoes. We

42
00:03:36.721 --> 00:03:41.149
were in such a rush, we left with only a carpetbag." "But

43
00:03:41.149 --> 00:03:45.180
where is your master going?" asked Fix. "He

44
00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:49.737
is going around the world in eighty days," said Passepartout. "He

45
00:03:49.737 --> 00:03:54.219
says it's a wager, but between you and me, I don't believe a word of it! I

46
00:03:54.219 --> 00:03:57.252
think he has another reason for the trip." "Ah!

47
00:03:57.252 --> 00:04:05.589
Mr. Fogg is a character, is he? Is he rich?" asked Fix as his eyes lit up. "No

48
00:04:05.589 --> 00:04:13.429
doubt," replied Passepartout. "He is carrying an enormous sum in brand-new banknotes with him." "And

49
00:04:13.429 --> 00:04:20.130
have you known your master long?" asked Fix, his voice filled with excitement. "Why,

50
00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:24.440
no, I entered his service the same day we left London. I

51
00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:30.066
will fetch him now and bring him to the consulate," Passepartout said. Passepartout's

52
00:04:30.066 --> 00:04:34.413
words had deeply intrigued the detective. Mr.

53
00:04:34.413 --> 00:04:38.911
Fogg had departed hastily from London, quite soon after the robbery. And

54
00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:45.814
the large sum of bank notes he carried was unusual, as was his peculiar eagerness to reach distant countries. All

55
00:04:45.814 --> 00:04:49.950
of it fit Fix's theory of the robber. Fix rapidly

56
00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:54.355
made his way to the British consulate. He was

57
00:04:54.355 --> 00:05:00.474
sure there would be a large reward for capturing such a bold criminal. The detective

58
00:05:00.474 --> 00:05:07.658
burst into the consul's office and exclaimed, "Consul, there's a thief on board the Mongolia. He will

59
00:05:07.658 --> 00:05:15.469
be here any moment. You must detain him!" Passepartout's Guidebook

60
00:05:15.469 --> 00:05:23.396
The race against time was on! We still had a long way to go, but luckily the Suez Canal had opened in 1869.

61
00:05:23.396 --> 00:05:29.193
The Suez Canal was the fastest way to go from Europe to India and Asia.

62
00:05:29.193 --> 00:05:34.411
Before it was built, goods had to be unloaded from ships and carried overland.

63
00:05:34.411 --> 00:05:40.682
Otherwise ships had to sail around all of Africa, and that was a rough and dangerous journey.

64
00:05:40.682 --> 00:05:43.147
I'm glad we didn't go that way!

65
00:05:43.147 --> 00:05:49.225
The Suez Canal is located in Egypt and links the Mediterranean Sea to the Red Sea.

66
00:05:49.225 --> 00:05:54.780
People think the ancient Egyptians first built a canal there nearly four-thousand years ago.

67
00:05:54.780 --> 00:06:00.018
Since then the canal has been destroyed, rebuilt, and destroyed again.

68
00:06:00.018 --> 00:06:07.035
Finally proper construction began in 1859, and the canal was completed in 1869.

69
00:06:07.035 --> 00:06:13.050
It is 101 miles long and about 984 feet wide at its narrowest point.

70
00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:18.536
Over 1.5 million people were forced to work on the canal, mostly by hand.

71
00:06:18.536 --> 00:06:25.980
Would you like to dig a canal by hand? I certainly wouldn't!

